# Chapter 8: Spell Check and Thesaurus.
.doc Strata: Spell Check and Thesaurus
.chapter 8
.h1 Spell Check and Thesaurus [spell]
.ix Spell check
.ix Spelling
.ix Thesaurus
.ix Synonyms
.ix Dictionary
.ix Tools menu, Spell
.ix Tools menu, Thesaurus
This chapter describes the two word tools of the Editor: the spell check
({k:F12}, Tools, Spell, and the key {k:Ctrl-C}) and the thesaurus ({k:F12}, Tools,
Thesaurus, and the key {k:Ctrl-T}).  Both look and behave the same in the
terminal version and in
the X11 version.

The chapter describes the tools one after the other: what the tools need,
then the spell check, then the thesaurus, then an example and a summary of
the keys.  The spell check section gives the commands of the Tools menu in
the order of the menu, the screen on which each unknown word is shown, the
dictionaries behind it, the messages and the limits.  The thesaurus section
gives the word, the panel, the keys, a word that is not found and the
replacement.

.table 24 36
Where | What it does
{k:F12}, Tools, Spell, Document | Checks the whole document.
{k:F12}, Tools, Spell, Block | Checks the blocks that you highlight.
{k:F12}, Tools, Spell, Section | Checks the sections that you highlight.
{k:F12}, Tools, Spell, Word | Checks the word at the cursor.
{k:Ctrl-C} | The same as Spell, Word.
{k:F12}, Tools, Thesaurus | Shows synonyms of the word at the cursor.
{k:Ctrl-T} | The same as Tools, Thesaurus.
.end

.h2 Requirements and files [spell-requirements]
.ix aspell
.ix hunspell
.ix STRATA_SPELL
.ix STRATA_THESAURUS
.ix mythes-en-us
.ix Personal word list
.ix ~/.strata/dictionary
The spell check does not contain a dictionary.  A checker program of the system
is used, {c:aspell} or {c:hunspell}, which names the words that are wrong and
suggests others.  The table gives what is needed and what is read.
.table 19 41
Item | What it is
{c:aspell} or {c:hunspell} | The spelling checker of the system.  {c:aspell} is
 | looked for first, then {c:hunspell}.  With neither, the
 | spell check shows a notice that shows what to install
 | ({n:spell-messages}).
{c:STRATA_SPELL} | An environment variable.  A program name or a path
 | that is used in place of {c:aspell} and {c:hunspell}.  When
 | it is set and is not a program, no other is tried.
{c:~/.strata/dictionary} | The personal word list of the user: plain text, one
 | word per line, UTF-8, written by the command Define and by
 | Maintain Dictionaries.
 | The environment variable {c:STRATA_HOME} names another
 | directory for it, as for the other settings.
Dictionary Language | A setting of the Document Manager's Setup, saved in
 | the settings of the user as {c:spell_language}.  The
 | default is en_US.  The value is passed to the checker.
mythes-en-us | The data of the thesaurus: the package of that name,
 | two files in {c:/usr/share/mythes} (an index and a data
 | file, {c:th_en_US_v2.idx} and {c:th_en_US_v2.dat}).
{c:STRATA_THESAURUS} | An environment variable.  A directory, the data file,
 | or its path without the suffix; it replaces
 | {c:/usr/share/mythes}.  Set to an empty or a wrong
 | value it switches the thesaurus off.
.end
Nothing of this is needed to edit documents: without a checker the spell check
reports this and the Editor goes on; without the data the thesaurus reports
this and the Editor goes on.

.h2 Spell check [spell-check]
.ix Tools, Spell
.ix F12, Tools, Spell
.ix Spell command
The spell check looks for words that are not in the dictionary, shows each
one in its text, and lets you correct it, keep it, add it to your own word
list or leave it.  The four commands are on the menu {k:F12}, Tools, Spell, in
this order; the letters are the initials, so {k:F12} {k:T} {k:S} and then
{k:D}, {k:B}, {k:S} or {k:W} choose one.  The line under the menu shows what
the highlighted command does.
.table 12 48
Command | What is checked
Document | The whole document, from the start to the end; the text of notes
 | is checked where their markers are.  Opens the panel Check
 | Document Spelling first.
Block | The blocks that you highlight.  The message line shows "Use arrow
 | keys to highlight block(s); then press Enter."  Enter then
 | opens the same panel.
Section | The sections that you highlight; the message line shows "Use
 | arrow keys to highlight section(s); then press Enter."
Word | The word at the cursor.  {k:Ctrl-C} does the same.
.end
Block and Section need the cursor in a block; otherwise the message line
shows "The cursor is not in a block".  The check works on the text in the
Editor, saved or not.

.pic pictures/spell-tools-menu.png 5.0 The Tools menu of the Editor with Spell and Thesaurus.

.h3 What a word is [spell-words]
.ix Word, in spell check
.ix Words with digits
.ix 65 letters
The text is cut into words at a space, a tab, a change of attribute and a
marker.  Punctuation at the end of a word is dropped; punctuation inside a
word stays, so that {c:qqa.qqb}, {c:qqc-qqd}, {c:qqf's} and {c:qqh,qqi} are
one word each.  A word with a digit inside it is always unknown (the
checker is not asked); a word that begins with a digit is skipped.  A word
of more than 65 letters is skipped without a message in a check of a
document or a block (the command Word rejects it, see {n:spell-word}).  A
replacement keeps the attributes (bold, underline) that the old word had.
.ix Attributes kept in a replacement
The text of a note (footnote, endnote) is checked where its marker stands;
the screen then names the place "Section: Body (Note)".  Index entries and
the text of equations are not checked.  The document is marked as changed
when a word was replaced.  Capitals do not matter for a word that is known.

.h3 Document [spell-document]
.ix Check Document Spelling panel
.ix Document Name
.ix Custom Spelling Dictionary
.ix Auto Correct?
{k:F12}, Tools, Spell, Document opens the panel Check Document Spelling.  In the
Editor the first three fields only show a value; only Auto Correct? can be
changed.
.table 24 36
Field | Meaning
Document Name | The name of the document as the Editor has it: the full path
 | when the Document Manager started the Editor (or "(not saved)").
Custom Spelling Dictionary | The personal word list that is in use,
 | {c:~/.strata/dictionary}.  There is one list for
 | the user.
Dictionary Language | The language code, such as en_US, and in
 | brackets the program that checks ({c:aspell} or
 | {c:hunspell}).  The language is set in the Setup of
 | the Document Manager.
Auto Correct? | Yes or No, default Yes.  With Yes, a
 | correction that you made once is made again by
 | itself when the same word comes later in the
 | check.  The choice is kept until the Editor ends.
.end
{k:Ins} accepts the panel and starts the check; {k:Esc} cancels it.  The
check stops at the first unknown word at once; a long document does not make
you wait for the whole walk through the text.  While the checker works and
finds nothing, the bar shows "Checking the spelling (Esc quits)" and
{k:Esc} ends the check.  What happens from there is described in
{x:spell-screen}.

.h3 Block [spell-block]
.ix Spell, Block
{k:F12}, Tools, Spell, Block prompts you to highlight the blocks with the arrow
keys as the commands of the Edit chapter that work on blocks do, then
{k:Enter} shows the panel Check Document Spelling and checks the highlighted
blocks.

.h3 Section [spell-section]
.ix Spell, Section
The same for sections: the highlight selects sections, and {k:Enter} then
shows the panel and checks them.

.h3 Word [spell-word]
.ix Spell, Word
.ix Ctrl-C, spell check of a word
.ix Spelled correctly
.ix Word is too long
{k:F12}, Tools, Spell, Word, and the key {k:Ctrl-C} anywhere in the text, check
the word at the cursor, with the same rules for what a word is as in
{x:spell-words}.  A box titled Spell Word shows the word and then one of
these.
.table 26 34
The box shows | When
Spelled correctly | The word is known.  Any key closes the box.
Not found in dictionary | The word is unknown and the checker has no
 | suggestion.  Any key closes the box.
Not found in dictionary, then | The word is unknown and there are
 Suggested alternatives: | suggestions, in a row.  {k:Left} and
 | {k:Right} choose; {k:Ins} or {k:Enter}
 | replaces the word in the document; {k:Esc}
 | cancels.
Word is too long - maximum length | The word has more than 65 letters.
 is 65 characters |
.end
The cursor can be anywhere in the word.  A replacement keeps the attributes
of the old word and marks the document as changed.  When the cursor is not
on a word the box shows "There is no word at the cursor".

.h2 The spell check screen [spell-screen]
.ix Spell check screen
.ix Accept
.ix Define
.ix Ignore
.ix Edit, in spell check
.ix Guess
.ix Line, in spell check
.ix Quit, in spell check
.ix Unknown word
When the check finds a word that is not known, the screen shows, from the
top: a bar of seven commands with a line of description below it, the line
"Unknown word:", the text round the word with the word highlighted, and at
the bottom lines that say where the word is: Document (the name), Section
(the headline, and "(Note)" in a note) and Dictionaries (the language and the
program, and the personal list that is in use).  The bar reads
.code
 Accept  Define  Ignore  Edit  Guess  Line  Quit
.end
A command is chosen with its first letter ({k:A}, {k:D}, {k:I}, {k:E},
{k:G}, {k:L}, {k:Q}) or by moving the highlight with {k:Left} and
{k:Right} and pressing {k:Return}.  The highlight starts on Accept.  The line
below the bar describes the highlighted
command.
.table 8 52
Command | What it does
Accept | "Accept word as correct".  Keeps the word as it is and does not
 | ask about it again in the rest of this check.  It is not written to
 | the personal list.
Define | "Define word in the personal dictionary".  Writes the word into
 | the personal word list at once, so that no later check puts a question about
 | it (a lower case word stands for the word with an initial capital
 | as well).
Ignore | "Ignore this word".  Leaves this occurrence and goes on to the
 | next unknown word.  The same word is asked about again if it comes
 | again.
Edit | "Edit the word".  Shows a line "Correct word:" with the word in
 | it, to edit with {k:Left}, {k:Right}, {k:Home}, {k:End},
 | {k:Backspace} and {k:Del}.  {k:Return} puts the word in place of
 | the old one and the check goes on; {k:Esc} goes back to the bar.
Guess | "List similar words".  Puts the suggestions of the checker on
 | the bar, at most twelve, and "Select word" below.  {k:Left},
 | {k:Right}, {k:Home} and {k:End} choose and {k:Return} takes the word;
 | {k:Esc} goes back to the bar.  With no suggestion the bar shows "No
 | alternatives found" and any key goes back.
Line | "Edit the line".  Opens a box Line with the field "Corrected line:"
 | that holds the line of the word; {k:Enter} accepts it, {k:Esc}
 | cancels.  A line with a tab or a marker cannot be changed this way
 | and the box shows "A tab or a marker cannot be changed here".
Quit | "Quit the spell check".  Ends the check.  If corrections were
 | made that can be repeated, a prompt reads "Continue with auto corrections?
 | Yes No" first.
.end

.pic pictures/spell-ctrl-c.png 5.5 The Spell Word box: the word, its suggestions and the keys.
.h3 Guess and the single suggestion [spell-guess]
.ix Accept correction?
.ix No alternatives found
.ix Select word
When the checker has exactly one suggestion and it differs from the word in
case only, the bar prompts "Accept correction? Yes No" and the line "Correct
word:" shows the suggestion, instead of the seven commands.  Yes replaces
the word and No leaves it; in both cases the check goes on.  After a
suggestion has been chosen the word is replaced in the text, with the
attributes of the old one, and the check goes on to the next unknown word.

.h3 Auto Correct [spell-autocorrect]
.ix Auto Correct
.ix Continue with auto corrections?
With the panel field Auto Correct? set to Yes, each correction that you make
(a suggestion, or an Edit) is remembered for the rest of the check, and the
same word is replaced by itself when it comes again, without a stop.  When
you choose Quit with such corrections stored, the question "Continue with
auto corrections?  Yes No" is asked: Yes makes the stored replacements in the
rest of the text, with no more questions, No leaves the rest.  When no
correction is stored, Quit ends the check at once.

.h3 The end of the check [spell-end]
.ix Spell Check completed
.ix Spell Check terminated
.ix No spelling errors found
.ix Press any key to exit
At the end a box appears.  Its first line is "Spell Check completed" when
the check ran to the end of the text, and "Spell Check terminated" when it
was ended with Quit.  A line "No spelling errors found" follows when nothing
was changed, defined or accepted (Ignore does not count as a change).  The
last line is "Press any key to exit".  The document is marked as changed (the
star in the status line) if a word was replaced; the check does not save the
document, {k:F12}, File, Save does.  The cursor stays where it was; if the word
it was in was replaced it is at the start of the new word.

.h2 Dictionaries [spell-dictionaries]
.ix Dictionaries
.ix Personal dictionary
.ix Dictionary Language
.ix Custom dictionary
There are two kinds of dictionary.  The main dictionary belongs to the
checker ({c:aspell} or {c:hunspell}) and is chosen by the setting Dictionary
Language of the Document Manager's Setup: a language code such as {c:en_US}
or {c:de_DE}.  The dictionary of that language must be installed for the
checker; when the checker does not start for the language, the notice
names the program and the language ({n:spell-messages}).

The personal word list is a text file, {c:~/.strata/dictionary}, with one
word per line, for you alone: Define adds a word to it, and every later
check uses it.  The file is
private to you.  A word in lower case also stands for the word with an
initial capital; any other entry is only that word.  To see the list, take a
word back out of it, or import and export words, use Maintain Dictionaries
({n:str-spell-words}).

.pic pictures/spell-str-setup.png 5.0 Setup of the Document Manager with the dictionary language.

.h2 Messages of the spell check [spell-messages]
.ix Spell check messages
.ix Messages, spell
.table 31 29
Message | When
Use arrow keys to highlight block(s); then press Enter. | Block, before the
 | highlight.
Use arrow keys to highlight section(s); then press Enter. | Section, before the
 | highlight.
The cursor is not in a block | Block or Section, with
 | the cursor outside a
 | block.
Unknown word: | The label on the check screen.
Correct word: | The field of Edit, and the
 | suggestion of the question
 | below.
Select word | The bar of suggestions
 | (Guess).
No alternatives found | Guess found nothing.
Accept correction?  Yes No | One suggestion that
 | differs in case only.
Continue with auto corrections?  Yes No | Quit, with corrections to
 | repeat.
Checking the spelling (Esc quits) | The checker is working and
 | has found nothing yet.
Spell Check completed | The check reached the end.
Spell Check terminated | The check was ended with
 | Quit.
No spelling errors found | Nothing was changed,
 | defined or accepted.
Press any key to exit | The last line of the end
 | box.
Corrected line: | The box of the command
 | Line.
A tab or a marker cannot be changed here | Line, for a line that has
 | one.
Spelled correctly | The word under {k:Ctrl-C} is
 | right.
Not found in dictionary | Spell Word: the word is
 | unknown.
Suggested alternatives: | Spell Word: the list.
There is no word at the cursor | Spell Word, with no word.
Word is too long - maximum length is 65 characters | Spell Word: a longer word.
The spelling checker stopped. | The checker program ended
 | in the middle of a check.
Save corrected document? | The end of a check in the spell
 | check mode of the Editor, after a change.
.end
Two notices are shown by the box titled Spell.  {c:No spelling checker was
found.  Install the packages hunspell and hunspell-en-us (or aspell and
aspell-en); the install script of Strata installs them.} appears when neither
program is found.  {c:The spelling checker hunspell did not start for the
language xx_YY: its dictionary is not installed.  Install the package
hunspell-xx-yy, ...} appears when the program is there and the dictionary of
the language is not (the name of the program, the language and the name of
the package are those of the case).  The Editor goes on after either.

.h2 Limits of the spell check [spell-limits]
.ix Spell check, limits
.table 13 47
Limit | State
Suggestions | The first twelve of the checker.
Line | A box with one field, not an edit in the text area.
Speed | The Editor does not wait long: a long text shows its first
 | unknown word at once.  Checking all of a long text with
 | every word ignored takes as long as the checker needs.
.end

.h2 Thesaurus [spell-thesaurus]
.ix Tools, Thesaurus
.ix F12, Tools, Thesaurus
.ix Thesaurus command
The thesaurus shows the synonyms of the word at the cursor.  {k:F12}, Tools,
Thesaurus and the key {k:Ctrl-T} do the same.  The word is highlighted in the
text, a panel opens over the lower or upper half of the screen, and one of
the synonyms can replace the word.  The data is read from the MyThes files
of the package mythes-en-us (see {n:spell-requirements}).

.pic pictures/spell-thesaurus-menu.png 5.0 The Tools menu with Thesaurus.

.h3 The word that is looked up [spell-thes-word]
.ix Word under the cursor
.ix No word selected
The word is the run of letters that holds the cursor, from the first letter
to the last: the cursor at the start of the word, in the middle of it, and
just after its last letter all give the same word.  An apostrophe or a
hyphen between two letters is inside the word ({c:don't}, {c:re-enter});
digits are not.  A change of attribute ends a word (so a bold part {c:sec}
of {c:section} is a word of its own and the highlight covers that part); a
marker ends a word.  If the cursor is on spaces, on a marker, after trailing
spaces or after a full stop, the lookup goes back along the same line to the
word before the cursor.  When the line has no word before the cursor (an
empty line, or the cursor in the leading spaces), a box titled Thesaurus shows
"No word selected".  A word longer than 63 letters is cut at 63.

.h3 The panel [spell-thes-panel]
.ix Thesaurus panel
.ix MORE
.ix Senses
The title of the panel is "Thesaurus".  The panel is twelve rows high and as
wide as the screen.  When the cursor is in the upper half of the screen, the
panel takes the bottom of the screen; when the cursor is lower, it takes the
top; on a screen of fewer than 25 rows it has half the rows of the screen.
The text is never scrolled to make room, so the line of the cursor is always
visible.  The rows, from the top of the panel:
.table 19 41
Part | Contents
First row | The word as it was typed, and a colon ("sections:"), and at the
 | right the word MORE with an arrow down, an arrow up, or both,
 | when there is more to see.
Senses | One block for each sense.  The part of speech (noun, adj, verb,
 | adv) is written at the first sense of that kind, right aligned
 | in four columns.  Each sense starts with a hyphen; the words are
 | separated by commas; a sense ends with a semicolon, the last
 | with a full stop; long lines wrap with a hanging indent.
Highlight | The first word is highlighted.
Bottom border | The hint "Enter:replace  Esc:exit  CTRL-T:look up"; after a
 | word has been looked up from the panel "Ctrl+PgUp:last word" is
 | added.
.end
Only synonyms are shown: the words of a sense and the words marked as similar
terms; antonyms, generic terms and related terms are not shown, and a sense
that has nothing to show is left out.  A word that has no sense left is
treated as not found.  A word may be in several senses.  At most 64 senses of
an entry are kept.

.pic pictures/spell-ctrl-t.png 5.5 The thesaurus panel for the word under the cursor.

.h3 Keys of the thesaurus panel [spell-thes-keys]
.ix Thesaurus keys
.ix Ctrl-PgUp
.ix Look Up box
.ix Esc, thesaurus
.table 15 45
Key | Effect
{k:Right}, {k:Left} | The next and previous word, over the ends of rows.  No
 | wrap at the first or last word.
{k:Down}, {k:Up} | The word of the next row down or up whose first column is
 | nearest.
{k:Home}, {k:End} | The first and last word of the row.
{k:PgDn}, {k:PgUp} | Scroll nine rows (no overlap) and highlight the first word
 | of the new top row; MORE shows the arrows.
{k:Ctrl-Home} | The word of the heading.
{k:Ctrl-End} | The last word on the screen.
{k:Enter} | Replace the word in the text by the highlighted word
 | ({n:spell-thes-replace}).
{k:Ctrl-T} | Look up the highlighted word; the words looked up before are
 | kept in a history of at most 20.
{k:Ctrl-PgUp} | Go back to the last word of the history.  The hint shows
 | while the history is not empty.
A letter | Opens the Look Up box with the letter in it.
{k:Esc} | Leave the thesaurus altogether, from any depth, with the text
 | unchanged and the cursor where it was.
.end
Keys that have no meaning in the panel are ignored.

.h3 The Look Up box [spell-thes-lookup]
.ix Look Up
Typing a letter in either panel opens a small box in the panel, titled Look
Up and holding the letter; its own hint is on its bottom border ("Esc:exit
CTRL-T:look up").  {k:Backspace} edits the word;
{k:Enter} or {k:Ctrl-T} looks it up; {k:Esc} closes only the box and goes
back to the panel.  A word of 70 letters can be typed.

.h3 A word that is not found [spell-thes-notfound]
.ix The word was not found
.ix Similar words
.ix Please choose another word
When the word has no entry (after trying its stems, see below) the panel is
titled "The word was not found -  Please choose another word" and lists ten
words of the dictionary in one column, in alphabetical order, five above the
highlighted word and four below.  The highlighted word is the
nearest one before the word looked up.  The list is not a spelling guess: it
lists the neighbors in the alphabet, not words of a small edit distance.
At the ends of the dictionary the list is shorter.  The words of the data
that have several words are in the list too.
.table 15 45
Key | Effect
{k:Up}, {k:Down} | Move by one word; the list scrolls at the edge.
{k:PgUp}, {k:PgDn} | A page of ten, the highlight staying at the same place.
{k:Enter}, {k:Ctrl-T} | Look up the highlighted word.
A letter | Starts a new look up in the Look Up box.
{k:Esc} | Leave.
.end
The hint on the bottom border is "Enter:look up  Esc:exit  CTRL-T:look up".

.h3 A word that has no synonyms [spell-thes-nosyn]
.ix The word has no synonyms
.ix Word without synonyms
Some words are in the data but have only generic terms or related terms, which
the thesaurus does not show: {c:zebra} has only the generic term "equine".
For such a word (after trying its stems) the panel is titled "The word has no
synonyms -  Please choose another word" and shows the same list of neighbors
as for a word that was not found, with the word itself highlighted; the keys
are the same as in {x:spell-thes-notfound}.  A word that is not in the data at
all still gives "The word was not found".

.h3 Inflected words [spell-thes-stems]
.ix Stemming
.ix Inflection
The data holds the base forms only.  The thesaurus therefore looks up the base
form of a word with a regular ending: {c:sections} finds {c:section},
{c:headlines} finds {c:headline} and {c:running} finds {c:run}.  The heading
still shows the word as you typed it.  An irregular form such as {c:ran} is
looked up as typed.

.h3 Replacing the word [spell-thes-replace]
.ix Replace with synonym
.ix Case of the replacement
{k:Enter} puts the highlighted word in place of the word of the text, and the
cursor stands after the new word.  The case of the old word is kept: a word
written with a capital first letter gives a word with a capital first letter
("Quick" gives "Center"), a word in capitals gives capitals ("QUICK" gives
"CENTER"), a word in lower case gives the word as the data has it; for a
synonym of several words only the first letter of the first word is changed
("Quick" gives "Next day").  The new word is not inflected: "sections"
becomes the base form that you chose, as it stands.  A bold word stays bold.
The document is marked as changed.  {k:Esc} changes
nothing.

.h3 When the data is missing [spell-thes-nodata]
.ix The thesaurus is not installed
Tools, Thesaurus and {k:Ctrl-T} work even without the data; they then
show a notice titled Thesaurus that shows "The thesaurus is not installed.
Install the package mythes-en-us; the install script of Strata installs it.",
and the Editor goes on.  A damaged index or data
file gives "not found" and never stops the Editor.

.h3 Limits of the thesaurus [spell-thes-limits]
.ix Thesaurus, limits
.table 26 34
Point | State
Word length | A word is cut at 63 letters; there is no "too long" error.
Panel on a screen of another height | Half the rows of the screen under 25
 | rows.
.end

.h2 A session step by step [spell-example]
.ix Example, spell check
.ix quikc
The text has the line "The quikc brown fox" and the checker is {c:aspell} with
the en_US dictionary.
.table 5 55
Step | What you do and what you see
1 | {k:F12}, {k:T}, {k:S}, {k:D}: the panel Check Document Spelling.  Auto
 | Correct? is Yes.  {k:Ins} starts the check.
2 | The screen shows the bar, "Unknown word: quikc" and the line with
 | {c:quikc} highlighted.  The letter {k:G} chooses Guess.
3 | The bar shows the suggestions, {c:quick} first, and "Select word" on
 | the description line.  {k:Right} moves to the next one,
 | {k:Return} takes the highlighted word.
4 | The word is replaced in the text, with the attributes it had, and
 | the check goes on to the next unknown word.  If {c:quikc} comes
 | again, Auto Correct?  Yes makes the same replacement by itself.
5 | At the end the box shows "Spell Check completed" and "Press any key
 | to exit".  The status line shows the star of a changed document.
.end
Another example, for the personal list: at an unknown word {c:wrold}, the
letter {k:D} chooses Define.  The word is written to the file
{c:~/.strata/dictionary} on its own line, and no later check, in any
document, stops at it.

.h2 Summary of the keys of this chapter [spell-keys]
.ix Keys of spell check and thesaurus
.table 12 14 24
Key | Where | What it does
{k:Ctrl-C} | Text | Check the word at the cursor.
{k:Ctrl-T} | Text | The thesaurus for the word at the cursor.
{k:F12}, {k:T}, {k:S} | Text | The Spell menu (Document, Block, Section, Word).
{k:Ins} | Panel | Start the check.
{k:Esc} | Panel | Cancel.
{k:A} {k:D} {k:I} {k:E} {k:G} {k:L} {k:Q} | Check screen | The seven commands, by initial.
{k:Left}, {k:Right}, {k:Return} | Check screen | Move the highlight on the bar, choose.
{k:Enter} | Thesaurus | Replace the word.
{k:Ctrl-T} | Thesaurus | Look up the highlighted word.
{k:Ctrl-PgUp} | Thesaurus | The last word looked up.
{k:Esc} | Thesaurus | Leave.
.end
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
.h2 The spell check mode of the Editor [spell-cmdline]
.ix --spell
.ix --auto
The Editor has a spell check mode that a script can use.  The options are
these.
.table 22 38
Option | Effect
{c:--spell FILE.doc} | Start the Editor on the file with the spell check shown at
 | once, and no edit screen.  At the end, if the document was
 | changed, a prompt reads "Save corrected document?"; Yes saves it.
{c:--auto} yes or no | With {c:--spell}: the value of Auto Correct? (default yes).
.end
Without a document the mode ends with status 2 and a message that a document
is needed; a document that cannot be read ends it with status 1; otherwise
the status is 0.
.endif
